        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "> Maybe the leave-a-cursor behavior should happen when follow-mode is on, otherwise not.\n\nI think it is the right workflow with a pinch of salt.\n\nIt does mask the issue: when follow-mode is enabled, ~~overlapping cursors are merged when moving~~ a cursor that overlaps with the primary one is merged on move:\n\n```\nV2jQ  -- set cursors, enables follow mode\n[C    -- 3 left after jump\nk[C   -- 2 left after jump\n```\n\nAlso, keeping the keymaps as dumb as possible (just jumping) has its advantages, they're easier to build custom behaviors on top of and compose into other mappings.\n\nAn imaginary API:\n\n<details open><summary>Click me</summary>\n\n```lua\nvim.keymap.set(\"n\", \"]C\", \"1Q]C\") -- 1Q to always set the cursor instead of toggling (!doesn't work this way)\nvim.keymap.set(\"n\", \"[C\", \"1Q[C\")\n\nvim.keymap.set(\"n\", \"]C\", \"v:is_follow ? '1Q]C' : ']C'\", { expr = true })\nvim.keymap.set(\"n\", \"[C\", \"v:is_follow ? '1Q[C' : '[C'\", { expr = true })\n```\n\n</details>\n\nThough I'd prefer a dedicated jump function, similar to `vim.diagnostic.jump()`, to solve the scripting problem **instead**, since it would be more flexible for both scripted and non-scripted use cases.",
