        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> It does mask the issue: when follow-mode is enabled, overlapping cursors are merged when moving:\n\nDo you mean the primary cursor overlapping? I think maybe you're right, and motion should never dedupe just by the primary overlap. \n\nWhereas if non-primary cursors overlap each other, they will never \"un-overlap\" on their own, so always dedupe in that case.\n\n\n\n> keeping the keymaps as dumb as possible (just jumping) has its advantages, they're easier to build custom behaviors on top of\n\nTrue 👍 \n\n\n\n> Though I'd prefer a dedicated jump function, similar to `vim.diagnostic.jump()`, to solve the scripting problem **instead**\n\nThat will all follow later (0.14 maybe), but it's a distraction for now. Need to nail down expectations first.",
